  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe deadline for filing an estate tax return is within one year from the decedent's death. An extension of up to 30 days may be granted by the Commissioner in meritorious cases.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 89**: \"The estate tax return required... shall be filed within one (1) year from the decedent's death.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nThe law mandates that the estate tax return must be filed within one year of the death of the decedent. However, the Commissioner of Internal Revenue has the discretion to extend this deadline by an additional 30 days if there are valid reasons presented by the taxpayer.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that executors or heirs must ensure that the estate tax return is prepared and submitted within the specified timeframe to avoid penalties. If circumstances warrant, they can request a short extension, but this must be justified to the Commissioner. It is advisable to plan ahead to meet these deadlines to ensure compliance with tax obligations.",
